A||||4||curtis||Battery dead||||5||
Z||000000||curtis||04-07-2001||05:26 PM||||I think something is draining my battery. at first it was having trouble turning over in the morning it would turn over slowly then finally crank. Then last weekend I didn't drive it at all and tried Monday and it was dead. Jumped it off Monday night and let it run for a while to charge up. Meter read 13 volts. Next morning it was dead again. Today I tried to jump it again, it had sat all week, and it wouldn't start. So  I decided to push start it when it finally started it had no power, meter showed low, dash lights were dim and hesitated bad. I replaced the batt about a year ago and replaced the alt. last month. Please help!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mark||04-07-2001||06:19 PM||||That battery or alternator could be bad.  First thing I would do is have the battery tested. Any place that sells batterys should have a load tester. Same for the alternator.  If you have a DC ampmeter, you could attempt to measure the battery current with everything off (and the battery fully charged).  I would guess it should be ony a few tens of milliamps at most.  Anything more, start pulling fuses to locate the current draw.<P>Oh yes, also check the obvious....battery terminals, cables and ground connections before you do anything!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-09-2001||10:39 AM||||I just had to replace a battery I got from WalMart in January.  They replaced it for free under warranty.  It happens sometimes.<P>I've been using their batteries for years and this is the first problem I've had.<P>When a battery is run completely dead it loses about half of it's useful life.  Popular Machanics had an excellent article on this a few months ago.  It showed the life expectancy of batteries after a number of deep cycle discharges.<P>When you jump start a car with a completely dead battery you are almost sure to fry the alternator if you simply disconnect the cables when the engine fires up (this is called Alternator death). You should leave the dead battery it hooked to the good battery for several minutes to transmit some charge to the dead battery before starting the engine.  Then leave the two hooked together for a little while to help minimize the shock on your alternator.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sevaan||04-09-2001||11:20 AM||||Or you might just have left something on. My battery was dead one morning so I feared that something was wrong with the alternator. But I charged up the battery anywhere and stuck it in the car and everything worked fine.<P>Then once I was driving again I realized that my interior roof lights were on. I must have turned them on when putting the dimmer switch on the dash to high. And I never realized it because I had been driving in day light for the past little while.||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||04-09-2001||12:36 PM||||Along the lines of the dome lights... another popular battery eater is "hidden" lights. If the switch on your hatch breaks and the trunk light stays on goodbye juice!||||reg||1
